# THE STANDALONE HOST IS A REAL RAILS APP AND WAS TRACKING ITS OWN RUNTIME STATE.
#
# This repo's root .gitignore is ENGINE-shaped -- /pkg/, /.bundle/, *.gem -- which is right for a
# gem and covers none of what an app underneath it produces. So host/ arrived with 15 runtime files
# committed: log/development.log, a server PID file, the development AND test sqlite3 databases
# with their -wal/-shm siblings, and -- the one that matters -- real bytes under
# tmp/chunk-store/chunks/. That last is user content by construction: this app's whole job is
# holding chunks, so a chunk that lands during a local run is exactly the thing that must never be
# committed.
#
# The practical cost until now was that ANY local `bin/rails server` left the tree dirty, so a
# `git commit -a` anywhere in this repo could sweep a database into a commit. Found while building
# the conformance corpus, which records against a live host and therefore runs it constantly.
/log/*
!/log/.keep
/tmp/*
!/tmp/.keep
# `!/tmp/pids/` BEFORE the contents rule, and the order is load-bearing: `/tmp/*` already ignored
# the pids DIRECTORY, and git will not descend into an ignored directory to un-ignore something
# inside it. Without this line `!/tmp/pids/.keep` silently does nothing -- git refuses the add and
# says the path is ignored.
!/tmp/pids/
/tmp/pids/*
!/tmp/pids/.keep
/storage/*
!/storage/.keep
